{"type":"message","id":"8306e89d","parentId":"f3bf370b","timestamp":"2026-05-17T01:12:43.761Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both authentication methods (X-API-Key and Bearer) gave different errors - X-API-Key got error 1101, Bearer got Unauthorized. Let me try GET first to see the correct auth header.\n\nActually, wait - the first curl calls with `X-API-Key` got error 1101, not Unauthorized. That means the authentication passed (the key was recognized) but there was a different error (code 1101). The Bearer header got 401 Unauthorized which means the key format was wrong.\n\nError 1101 is likely a Cloudflare error (1101: \"Worker threw exception\" or similar). But actually looking at it, the `X-API-Key` header was accepted (no 401), the request made it through to the worker but the worker threw an error. \n\nSo X-API-Key is the right format, but the body content might be causing the issue. Let me try stripping out the newlines from the message body.\n\nActually, I'm noticing something odd - the POST request with X-API-Key returned error 1101, which is a Worker exception, but the GET requests with the same header returned \"Unauthorized\" instead. That's inconsistent. Looking back at the GET attempts, Bearer gave 401 Unauthorized, but X-API-Key initially returned \"Unauthorized\" before succeeding later. So the authentication format seems to work sometimes but not others, which suggests the header format itself might be the problem.\n\nNow looking at the POST responses, X-API-Key returned error 1101 (Worker threw exception) while Bearer returned 401 Unauthorized. The 1101 error is particularly strange - it indicates the worker encountered an exception, but the authentication technically worked since it got past the auth check. The issue might not be the API key itself but something in the request body or how it's being processed.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","